egresslock teardown <profile|all>
egresslock teardown --runtime

Remove the profile's containers (the anchor, and the gateway for a
gateway profile) and its network. The nftables table stays behind;
`teardown --runtime` deletes it, except when a kit network is kept
in use — then the table is kept too, so that workload stays policed.

`teardown all` tears down the main profile only: it reads main.conf
and does not scan every profile.

`teardown --runtime` needs no config: it finds and removes the
containers, networks, and firewall table this kit created, by name
(both the current and older container/network names). Use it when
the config file is already gone.

If a listing fails, the sweep names what it could not see,
prints `incomplete sweep`, and exits 1; a failed removal or a
kept network still exits 0 with its own note. A kept network also
keeps the firewall table — remove the workload and re-run to
delete it.

Arguments and flags:
  <profile|all>    profile to tear down, or `all` for main.conf only
  --runtime        sweep without a config; takes no other argument

Example:
  egresslock teardown main

Exit codes:
  0  success
  1  incomplete --runtime sweep, or fail closed (the message names the cause)
  2  usage error
EOF
            ;;
        config)
            cat <<'EOF'
Profile configuration

Profiles, rules, and gateways come from a strictly-validated
declarative config file. A config is REQUIRED: the engine has no
compiled-in profiles. One unix account has ONE config directory,
always <account-home>/.config/egresslock (the account home comes from
the passwd database, not the $HOME variable, so a different HOME
cannot move the config directory). Each profile lives in
<account-home>/.config/egresslock/<profile>.conf, and the file's
basename MUST equal the single `profile` it defines (one profile per
file). A gateway profile's allowlist must be the conf's co-located
`<profile>-allowlist`; its `gateway <ip> <port> <file>` line names
exactly that file. Any other basename, an absolute path, or a path
with a directory component is rejected at load (exit 2, naming the
line and the expected file), so the line can never name another
profile's file. A symlink or hardlink placed at
`<profile>-allowlist` inside the account's own confdir is an explicit
operator act and is not detected. A profile subcommand without its
file exits 2 with
`no profile config`. `list` and `verify --ensured` aggregate every
*.conf there; `teardown all` reads main.conf only.
`teardown --runtime` is the exception: it loads NO
conf at all and sweeps kit containers/networks of BOTH name
generations by name scan (the recovery when the conf is already
gone). Prints exactly ONE stderr resolution line on every
conf-resolving invocation (every time, TTY-independent): `using
config <path>` for a named profile, or `using configs in <dir>
(N profiles)` for a command that covers several profiles.
See examples/main.conf for a worked config
grammar example.

Which check answers which question — four commands, four layers:
  checks:
    egresslock doctor                      host env (podman/nft/userns)
    egresslock-setup doctor [--account]    kit/account wiring
    egresslock-setup apparmor check        pasta AppArmor patch
    egresslock verify <profile>            profile runtime (fail-closed)
EOF
